  [3mTrade-off:[0m rebuilding the whole list on every keystroke-free mutation is wasteful in principle. At tens-to-hundreds of
  tasks it's imperceptible, and it eliminates the entire class of "UI drifted out of sync with state" bugs.

  [1mB. Direct DOM manipulation, events as an afterthought[0m
  Each handler updates both the DOM node and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m directly. Fastest to write for the first three features.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m two sources of truth. Every new feature has to remember to update both, and the change-event chokepoint has
  no natural home — you end up emitting events from scattered handlers, which is exactly the retrofit problem we agreed
  to avoid. I'd rule this out.

  [1mC. Store-with-subscribers, targeted DOM updates[0m
  Same store as A, but the renderer patches only affected nodes instead of rebuilding.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m strictly better performance, meaningfully more code — you hand-maintain the mapping from task id to DOM
  node, including add/remove/reorder. It's A plus a manual optimization. Worth doing [3mif[0m a list ever gets slow, not
  before.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m It's barely more code than B, it gives the change-event chokepoint a single obvious home, and C is
  a later optimization of A rather than a different design — so choosing A doesn't foreclose C.

  Before I present the full design in sections: since this is effectively a new project with no tooling configured, this
  is the cheapest moment to decide what to set up.

